{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "cd ./rev/rev_crushing; python3 << 'EOF'\nimport struct\n\ndata = open('message.txt.cz','rb').read()\n\n# From main disasm:\n# - map is array of 255 pointers (0xff = 255 entries!), each entry a linked list\n# - main: rep stosq zeroes 0xff (255) entries = 255*8 bytes = 2040 bytes map region [rbp-0x810]\n#   Wait, 0x810 = 2064 bytes region. 0xff * 8 = 2040. Hmm but loop count 0xff=255, not 256.\n#   Actually rep stosq with ecx=0xff stores 255 qwords. So map has 255 entries (chars 0-254).\n#   Actually loop bound in serialize is 0xfe (254). So char range 0-254.\n#\n# - add_char_to_map(map, char_index, position): \n#     walks to end of linked list map[char], appends node {value=position, next=0}\n#     So each map[char] is a linked list of positions where char appears.\n#\n# - serialize: for each char i in 0..0xfe:\n#     writes list_len(map[i]) as 8 bytes  -> count of occurrences of char i\n#     then for each node writes node->value as 8 bytes  -> position\n#\n# So message.txt.cz format:\n#   First: 255 * 8 bytes = 2040 bytes of counts? No wait - serialize writes count then nodes per char.\n#   So layout: for each char 0..254 (255 chars):\n#      8 bytes count\n#      8*count bytes positions\n#\n# Let me parse that.\n\n# 0x50 = 80. Hmm but our file starts with 80 zero bytes. That doesn't match \"counts\".\n\n# Wait. Let me reconsider. The region [rbp-0x810] is 2064 bytes. If map has 255 entries * 8 = 2040, fits.\n# But maybe the map array indexing uses char as byte 0-255 but we only do chars that appear.\n# serialize loop bound: cmp dword [rbp-4], 0xfe ; jle = while i <= 0xfe, i.e., i in 0..0xfe = 255 values.\n\n# So output = for i from 0 to 254: [count_i (8 bytes)][pos_1..pos_count (8 bytes each)]\n# Total output for counts = 255*8 = 2040 bytes.\n\n# Let's verify: 0x50 = 80. Hmm 80 != expected count positions.\n\n# Wait the file starts with 0x50=80 zero bytes. That's odd. Maybe there's a header of 16 bytes (first count=0 for char 0?) \n\n# Actually maybe the counts region begins at 0. Let me check: at 0, 8 bytes=0 (count of char 0 = 0, since char 0 NUL doesn't appear)...\n# Then 8 bytes... Let me parse counts serially and see if positions match the observed 0x50 offsets.\n\n# Let's parse: for each char i, read 8-byte count at cursor. If 0, next. \nEOF\npython3 << 'EOF'\nimport struct\ndata = open('message.txt.cz','rb').read()\nprint(\"file len:\", len(data))\n\n# Parse assuming: cursor over serialized counts+positions\ncursor = 0\npositions_map = {}\nfor i in range(255):  # 0..0xfe\n    if cursor+8 > len(data): print(\"OOB at char\",i); break\n    cnt = struct.unpack('<Q', data[cursor:cursor+8])[0]\n    cursor += 8\n    poss = []\n    for j in range(cnt):\n        p = struct.unpack('<Q', data[cursor:cursor+8])[0]\n        pos += 0  # noop\n        poss.append(struct.unpack('<Q', data[cursor:cursor+8])[0])\n        cursor += 8\n    if cnt: positions_map[i] = poss\n    if cnt: print(f\"char {i} (0x{i:02x} '{chr(i) if 32<=i<127 else '.'}'): count={cnt} firstpos={poss[0] if poss else '-'}\")\nprint(\"total cursor after parse:\", cursor, \"file len:\", len(data), \"remaining:\", len(data)-cursor)\ncnt_total = sum(len(v) for v in positions_map.values())\nprint(\"total chars counted:\", cnt_total)\nEOF"}, "call_id": "call_00_XAYhwo6OIkKzoYwyThwc9470"}}
