{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "cd /workspace/g-11 && python3 -c \"\nfrom pwn import *\nfrom capstone import *\nimport warnings\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\ne = ELF('./delulu')\n# banner 0x13f2 and 0x144a setup already known.\n# The key insight: main does read(0, rbp-0x30, 0x1f) reading 31 bytes,\n# then printf(rbp-0x30) format string vuln.\n# rbp-0x38 holds ptr to challenge (rbp-0x40). So we want %n write to change.\n# Use fmtstr to write 0x1337beef low bytes to the dword pointed by [rbp-0x38]=&(rbp-0x40).\n# But PIE is on. However the ptr to challenge rbp-0x38 is a STACK address, stable.\n# The %n needs a target pointer value. We can use the pointer stored at rbp-0x38 on stack.\n# We provide format where one of the positional args points to the challenge.\n# Challenge value is 0x1337babe. Need to write 0x1337beef.\n# Low 2 bytes: 0xbabe -> 0xbeef (delta 0xbeef - 0xbabe = 0x235) small!\n# High: 0x1337 stays. Actually as dword: currently 0x1337babe, want 0x1337beef.\n# So write 2 bytes 0xbeef at low. Or %hn writing 0xbeef to offsets.\n\n# Let's do fmtstr. The value rbp-0x38 contains pointer to challenge. So %N$n where arg N is the stack slot holding rbp-0x38 (ptr) would write to challenge.\n# fmtstr_payload with writes {challenge_addr: 0x1337beef} won't have address since PIE.\n# Instead write 2 bytes: target = [rbp-0x38]. We need to reference the slot that holds the pointer.\n# The buffer starts at rbp-0x30 = our format string stored. We know buffer start addr.\n# First 8 bytes of buffer could contain the pointer to challenge... but we get ptr from rbp-0x38.\n# Simplest: the stack contains rbp-0x38 as a qword pointer. In printf the args start at rbp-0x.. \n# Let's determine offset. We'll run locally.\n\nprint('Disabling ASLR locally to test offsets')\n# run the binary with glibc loader locally\n\" 2>&1 | grep -v '^\\[*\\]'"}, "call_id": "call_00_bfAJ6kuZdWxB0IM9ViBS6396"}}
