{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "cd /workspace/g-14/extracted_maybe/rev_flecksofgold; python3 << 'EOF'\n# The flag is written to FlagPart buffer. rbp at 0x54b0 = r14 + r12*2 (entity's byte pair).\n# byte [rbp]  = index (movsx rax, byte ptr [rbp])\n# byte [rbp+1]= char value in flag\n# Then writes to [rdx+rax] = index position in FlagPart buffer.\n# So entity name first byte = flag index, second byte = flag char.\n# BUT entity names are like \"Explorer\",\"Armanix\"... these are std::string.\n# In flecs the entity name is a 2-char fragment?? No - the string is full name.\n# Wait - earlier I found names at 0xc546b. But those are the descriptive \"Explorer has found a flag part\" strings.\n# The ACTUAL flag fragments must be stored elsewhere. Let me re-examine.\n# r14 = [rdx+8] where rdx = [rsp+0x68] (same for all iterations, it's the query data).\n# rbp = r14 + r12*2 where r12 is row index. So r14 rows are 2-byte records.\n# This means the flag fragment table r14 is 2 bytes per entity: (index, char).\n# Let me find where r14 (this 2-byte table) comes from in main().\n# Look at main @0x3af0 to see how entities are created with 2-byte names.\nimport capstone\nd=open('flecks','rb').read()\nmd=capstone.Cs(capstone.CS_ARCH_X86, capstone.CS_MODE_64)\n# disassemble main @0x3af0\ncode=d[0x3af0:0x3af0+0x1800]\nfor i in md.disasm(code, 0x3af0):\n    # look for entity.name() / flecs::entity creation, or string references\n    print(\"0x%x:\\t%s\\t%s\" % (i.address, i.mnemonic, i.op_str))\nEOF"}, "call_id": "call_00_LYFTVbtHqn864G52uFKp0584"}}
