{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "cd /workspace/g-14/extracted_maybe/rev_flecksofgold; echo \"=== entities created via .set/.add in main — search for the byte-pair table & entity names in rodata ===\"\n# The r14 table at lambda 0x53a2 is [rbx+8] = Person's second field. \n# Person struct: [rbx] is one field, [rbx+8] is another. r14=[rbx+8].\n# The fragment pairs come from Person's data. \n# At main, look for where Person entities are built with .set<Person>(...) or set_entity.\n# Search /tmp/main.txt for the rodata addresses where entity names point.\npython3 << 'EOF'\n# Find what data r14 (the byte-pair table) actually is.\n# In the lambda, r14=[rbx+8]. rbx is the Person. So Person is a 2-field struct:\n#   [rbx+0] = integer (index/len?)\n#   [rbx+8] = pointer to byte-pair table (the flag fragment data)\n# So each Person entity carries a pointer to its (index,char) fragment data.\n# The names Explorer/Armanix etc are entity NAMES (used in print), separate.\n# The byte-pair table is referenced by Person+8 = the second field set in main via .set<Person>.\n\n# So in main, when creating each Person entity, .set<Person>({something, byte_table_ptr})\n# happens. The byte_table_ptr points to rodata arrays like [idx,char,0,0].per entity.\n# Let's find these arrays in rodata. Look for data right after the name strings.\n# Names are at 0xc5190? Explore rodata near c54xx.\n\nd=open('flecks','rb').read()\n# VA of rodata? Use readelf\nEOF\necho \"--- readelf sections ---\"\nreadelf -S flecks 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"rodata|\\.text|\\.data\" "}, "call_id": "call_00_kNCJ5KcJft2F4IMkfVBy5717"}}
