{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "cd /workspace/g-14/extracted_maybe/rev_flecksofgold; python3 << 'EOF'\nd=open('flecks','rb').read()\n# The pair table is NOT the name string itself. r14 is a separate pointer stored in the Person entity via .set<Person>.\n# In lambda: 0x5437 mov r14, [rdx+8] ; rdx=[rsp+0x68], rbx=[rdx] , r14=[rdx+8].\n# rbx = one field, r14 = second field of Person. Person component has 2 fields.\n# Then rbp = r14 + r12*2. r12 is the pair index within the CURRENT entity's byte table.\n# So r14 points to that entity's OWN byte-pair array. Each entity has its own array of {idx,char}!\n# The entity NAME (Armanix etc) is just for display. The actual byte data is in a SEPARATE static array passed to .set<Person>.\n# So I need to find, in main, the .set<Person>( {pos,...} ) calls that pass the byte-pair array pointers.\n\n# Let me find Person.set calls in main. Person is a 2-field? Actually let me look at where each entity is created: probably world.entity().set<Person>(...)  or world.set<Person>(entities[i], {...}).\n# The byte tables would be named data arrays in rodata. Let me search for the actual flag char sequences.\n# The flag chars are printable ASCII. A byte-pair array looks like: [idx small, ascii, ...]. \n# But each entity has its OWN array. Let me search rodata for arrays that are running (idx,ascii) pairs but SMALL-index (idx < 0x40).\n\n# Let me search entire section for the person byte data - find 2-byte-aligned runs producing readable text.\nimport re\n# Scan rodata for byte patterns. Each entity's table should decode to a readable string when pairs -> chars.\n# But chars here are single flag chars (not full words). Each entity contributes several fragments.\n# Let me instead look at main where entities get set. Let me dump main around entity creation (earlier part 0x3fxx-0x52xx).\nimport capstone\nmd=capstone.Cs(capstone.CS_ARCH_X86, capstone.CS_MODE_64)\n# find 'lea' with rodata ptr in main\nbin=d[0x3af0:0x5