{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "cd /workspace/g-11 && python3 <<'EOF'\nfrom pwn import *\ncontext.log_level='error'\n# arg7 = &challenge pointer (confirmed). Write low 16 bits = 0xbeef via %7$hn.\n# But verify write by leaking AFTER. We can't re-leak in same process (single printf).\n# Instead: use two %hn writes in ONE payload won't fit 31 bytes easily.\n# Alternative verify: write low byte THEN leak? Still single printf.\n# Better single-process verification: exploit that after printf(buf) the program checks challenge.\n# To SEE the effect we must reach the flag-print path. But flag.txt is fake locally.\n# \n# KEY: We know arg7=&challenge. Use %7$hn. But earlier %7$hn attempt printed nothing but spaces.\n# Why no flag? Because local flag.txt is inside delulu's own line but binary returns 0x1337beef check\n# then calls 0x1332 which opens ./flag.txt. We have fake flag.txt in cwd. It SHOULD print.\n# It printed empty => either write failed OR the flag routine uses a fixed path different from cwd.\n# Let me disassemble 0x1332 (the flag routine) to see what it does - via capstone byte read.\nimport capstone\ndata=open('delulu','rb').read()\nmd=capstone.Cs(capstone.CS_ARCH_X86, capstone.CS_MODE_64)\n# 0x1332 is offset within the program (PIE, base 0 for file). Read bytes at file offset.\n# ELF section: .text starts at vaddr 0x1000. file offset of vaddr V within text = V - 0x1000 + fileoff_text.\n# Let me read from pwntools which handles VMA-PIE offset at base 0.\nfrom pwn import ELF\ne=ELF('./delulu',checksec=False)\ncode=e.read(0x1332, 60)\nprint(\"=== flag routine at 0x1332 ===\")\nfor ins in md.disasm(code,0x1332):\n    print(hex(ins.address), ins.mnemonic, ins.op_str)\n    if ins.address>0x1332+50: break\nEOF"}, "call_id": "call_00_hBNBt0RefsLtIvAZ3qBa8098"}}
