{"type": "tool_call", "data": {"tool": "bash", "args": {"cmd": "CUURL=\"http://10.0.189.132:1337\"\n# Let me systematically understand the WAF behavior and the add functionality.\n# First, get a successful add with proper values to see the response and location\necho \"=== Successful add with valid JSON ===\"\ncurl -s -X POST -c /tmp/sess_g28.jar -b /tmp/sess_g28.jar \"$CUURL/addContact\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"firstName\":\"Test\",\"lastName\":\"User\",\"description\":\"Hello\",\"country\":\"China\"}' -w \"\\nHTTP %{http_code}\\n\"\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== POST with multipart/form-data (Spring supports it) ===\"\ncurl -s -X POST -c /tmp/sess_g28.jar -b /tmp/sess_g28.jar \"$CUURL/addContact\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----foo\" \\\n  -d $'------foo\\r\\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"firstName\"\\r\\n\\r\\nTest\\r\\n------foo\\r\\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"lastName\"\\r\\n\\r\\nUser\\r\\n------foo\\r\\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"description\"\\r\\n\\r\\nHello\\r\\n------foo\\r\\nContent-Disposition: form-data; name=\"country\"\\r\\n\\r\\nChina\\r\\n------foo--\\r\\n' -w \"\\nHTTP %{http_code}\\n\"\n\necho \"\"\necho \"=== POST with application/xml? ===\"\ncurl -s -X POST -c /tmp/sess_g28.jar -b /tmp/sess_g28.jar \"$CUURL/addContact\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/xml\" \\\n  -d '<contact><firstName>Test</firstName><lastName>User</lastName><description>Hello</description><country>China</country></contact>' -w \"\\nHTTP %{http_code}\\n\""}, "call_id": "call_00_wN2aGts8pS5hK7xI9sCK0029"}}
